            <section>
               <title value="Hospital course note"/>
               <code>
                  <coding>
                     <system value="http://loinc.org"/>
                     <code value="8648-8"/>
                     <display value="Hospital course note"/>
                  </coding>
               </code>
               <text>
                  <status value="generated"/>
                  <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the recent hospital admission of your patient, a 69-year-old male, who presented with an acute exacerbation of his known chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. On admission, he exhibited increased dyspnoea, productive cough, and reduced oxygen saturation. Chest X-ray showed pulmonary hyperinflation without evidence of pneumonia or pneumothorax. Arterial blood gas analysis confirmed hypoxaemic respiratory insufficiency. Blood tests revealed mild systemic inflammation. The patient was treated with inhaled bronchodilators via nebuliser, systemic corticosteroids, and supplemental oxygen therapy. Clinical monitoring included continuous pulse oximetry and regular vital sign assessments. He responded well to treatment with significant symptomatic improvement. He is discharged in stable condition and I recommend continuation of optimised inhaler therapy and close outpatient follow-up. Yours sincerely, Dr. [Treating Physician], Department of Internal Medicine</div>
               </text>
            </section>
